# Project 4 — Quantitative Structural Comparison of Lysozyme

## Overview

This project extends the earlier structural analysis of hen egg-white lysozyme by performing a quantitative comparison between two experimentally determined structures.

The structures compared were:

- **1AKI** — free hen egg-white lysozyme
- **1FDL** — hen egg-white lysozyme bound to the Fab D1.3 antibody fragment

The aim was to determine how similar the two structures are at the global structural level.

## Research Question

How structurally similar is hen egg-white lysozyme when it is free compared with when it is bound to an antibody?

## Structures Compared

| Feature | 1AKI | 1FDL |
|---|---|---|
| Protein | Hen egg-white lysozyme | Hen egg-white lysozyme in Fab D1.3 complex |
| Chain | A | C (author chain Y) |
| Residues | 1–129 | 1–129 |
| Experimental method | X-ray diffraction | X-ray diffraction |
| Resolution | 1.50 Å | 2.50 Å |

## Tools Used

- RCSB Protein Data Bank
- RCSB Pairwise Structure Alignment
- TM-align
- Protein structure analysis

## Method

1. Retrieved the structures of 1AKI and 1FDL from the RCSB Protein Data Bank.
2. Selected the hen egg-white lysozyme chain from each structure.
3. Compared residues 1–129 of the two lysozyme structures.
4. Used the RCSB pairwise structure alignment interface with TM-align.
5. Recorded RMSD, TM-score, sequence identity and the number of aligned residues.
6. Interpreted the structural similarity in a biological context.

## Quantitative Results

| Measure | Result |
|---|---:|
| RMSD | 0.37 Å |
| TM-score | 0.99 |
| Sequence identity | 100% |
| Aligned residues | 129 / 129 |
| Length compared | 129 / 129 |

## Interpretation

The RMSD of **0.37 Å** is very small, indicating that the corresponding atoms in the two aligned structures are positioned very similarly.

The **TM-score of 0.99** indicates extremely high global structural similarity.

All **129 residues** were aligned and the compared sequences showed **100% sequence identity**.

Together, these results indicate that the overall three-dimensional fold of hen egg-white lysozyme is highly conserved between the free structure and the antibody-bound structure.

## Biological Significance

The quantitative comparison supports the observations made in the earlier structural projects.

Although the lysozyme in 1FDL is bound to an antibody, its overall three-dimensional structure remains highly similar to the free lysozyme structure represented by 1AKI.

This suggests that antibody binding does not cause a major change in the global fold of the protein.

## Important Caveat

A very low global RMSD does not mean that every residue is completely unchanged.

Local conformational changes may still occur, particularly around the antibody-binding interface.

In the 1FDL structure, antibody contacts with lysozyme include residues **18–27** and **117–125**.

A residue-level analysis would therefore be useful for determining whether small local structural changes occur despite the very high global similarity.

## What I Learned

This project helped me move from qualitative protein structure visualization to quantitative structural analysis.

I learned:

- What RMSD represents
- How TM-score is used to evaluate structural similarity
- How sequence identity relates to structural comparison
- How to interpret aligned residue counts
- Why global structural metrics should be interpreted together with biological context

## Limitations

This analysis focused on global structural similarity.

It did not include:

- Residue-level distance analysis
- Backbone-angle comparison
- Interface energetics
- Detailed conformational analysis of antibody-contacting residues

## Next Step

A useful follow-up would be to examine the antibody-contacting residues individually and compare their local geometry between 1AKI and 1FDL.

This could determine whether small local structural changes are present despite the extremely high global structural similarity.

